{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"4f366f8a4238e805","type":"group","x":-1360,"y":800,"width":1090,"height":720,"color":"1","label":"IP Enrichment"},
		{"id":"31572117f89f3f2d","type":"group","x":-1440,"y":1760,"width":1040,"height":680,"color":"#666300","label":"Services Exposure"},
		{"id":"d6e03346ed3cc2ce","type":"group","x":-1300,"y":-100,"width":1060,"height":540,"color":"5","label":"Domain Enrichment"},
		{"id":"b6f1a314eab640b7","type":"group","x":-2400,"y":1160,"width":920,"height":480,"color":"#ffffff","label":"VPN Detection"},
		{"id":"1f49cd16edfa2607","type":"text","text":"# File and Hash Threat Intel","x":-200,"y":-240,"width":380,"height":60},
		{"id":"ea2ad7350d9535a3","type":"text","text":"# IP and Domain Threat Intel","x":-640,"y":-240,"width":370,"height":60},
		{"id":"109c8242b7c870ca","type":"text","text":"### Resolving DNS Domains:\n- [nslookup.io](https://www.nslookup.io/) \n- [dnschecker.org](https://dnschecker.org/)\n> **check**:\n> - *A / AAAA Records* (see if it is tied to a known APT infrastructure or maps to a known attack campaign.)\n>   \n>   - *TXT Records* (an attack on infrastructure will generally have empty TXT records or point you to some interesting discoveries, such as suspicious SPF records or a faked DKIM signature, often abused during phishing campaigns.)\n\n","x":-820,"y":-55,"width":400,"height":215,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"a01e69157f817cc8","type":"text","text":"### WHOIS / RDAP:\n- [WHOIS](https://whois.domaintools.com/) (gradually being replaced by RDAP) (most importantly, the domain's age. Modern attack infrastructure rarely stays online for more than a few months, since it is either taken down or rotated to avoid detection)\n  \n- \n","x":-640,"y":240,"width":380,"height":180,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"6caed489642e002f","type":"file","file":"Attack Techniques Using DNS.md","x":-1260,"y":-55,"width":400,"height":400,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"7b9f11b0c5de3c92","type":"text","text":"we got the hash: \n```\nPowerShell: Get-Filehash -Algorithm SHA256 bl0gger.exe\n```\n\n```\nubuntu@tryhackme$ sha256sum bl0gger.exe\n```\n\nA few pointers as analysts when dealing with hashes:\n\n- Store the hashes in lowercase to avoid needless differences.\n- Hash what matters in your investigation. For example, if the malware resides in a ZIP file, hash both the archive and the extracted binary.\n- Do not leave plain strings without the context of where and when you encountered them.\n- Any byte change will change the resulting hash.","x":-17,"y":-80,"width":525,"height":170},
		{"id":"6b4e8ce83d28d969","type":"text","text":"### Sandboxing Tools:\n-  [**Hybrid Analysis**](https://hybrid-analysis.com/) --> focuses on behaviour trees and a clean MITRE ATT&CK heatmap. It is suitable for a fast executive summary.\n- [**Joe Sandbox**](https://www.joesandbox.com/) -->goes deep, covering system calls, strings, and memory dumps. Great for reverse engineers and detection engineers\n\n____\n","x":38,"y":620,"width":567,"height":200},
		{"id":"57b92df2784697bb","type":"text","text":" [VirusTotal](https://www.virustotal.com/)\n ","x":55,"y":160,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"d171bc273de59b1c","type":"text","text":"[MalwareBazaar](https://bazaar.abuse.ch/) (all-in-one database for malware collection and analysis)\n\n>You will find files classified by their malware families. An example of this is a file with only 5/70 detections on VirusTotal but tagged as `icedID` in MalwareBazaar; it should be treated as malicious.","x":163,"y":320,"width":387,"height":180},
		{"id":"f55d031b470c0afb","type":"text","text":"- [AbuseIPDB](https://www.abuseipdb.com/): To see if the IP was involved in any port scans or brute-force attacks\n  \n- [VirusTotal](https://www.virustotal.com/): To get the overall IP reputation and read its community comments (if any)","x":-800,"y":820,"width":420,"height":130},
		{"id":"b42e409d2fcd7bf3","type":"text","text":" [BGP.Tools](https://bgp.tools/)  \n website would give you the most comprehensive info about the AS and its purpose. Looking at the ASN helps analysts assess the likely role of an IP","x":-760,"y":1080,"width":470,"height":140},
		{"id":"fdf07450aaa799ab","type":"text","text":"**==🔴NOTE==:**  if the IP is not within the CDN range, even 1 detection is an indicator of a risky IP","x":-1285,"y":575,"width":345,"height":90,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"4bb0e22594308e7a","type":"text","text":"- [ipinfo.io](https://ipinfo.io/)  \n- [iplocation.net](https://iplocation.net/)\n\n\n**geolocation lookup helps you with**:\n- **User logon analysis**: If a US-based employee logs on from the Netherlands, it might indicate an intrusion\n- **Network analysis**: If you monitor a local European company, any traffic to, let's say, Vietnam is suspicious","x":-680,"y":1300,"width":390,"height":180},
		{"id":"f2b11a35f2be76b1","type":"file","file":"Autonomous Systems.md","x":-1260,"y":820,"width":400,"height":400},
		{"id":"fc9c0cd72782559d","type":"text","text":"## Exposed Services\n\nKnowing what services are exposed on an IP is useful from two angles. The **first** is when you're looking at a victim's public IP address and trying to figure out how the attacker got in. ==🟢If SSH is exposed, it's likely a starting point==. The **second** is when you look up the attacker's IP. ==If it runs outdated services or has RDP exposed, the host was likely compromised and is being used as a jump point by attackers==.","x":-1360,"y":1780,"width":425,"height":300},
		{"id":"f46357e5de6e7e0f","type":"text","text":"- [Shodan](https://www.shodan.io/):\n  > we can extract information on:\n- **Open Ports**: This is the first fingerprint of exposure. Both Nginx and Rsync can be targeted by threat actors.\n  \n- **Service Banners**: These provide hints on service versions. An old version usually means a vulnerable version.","x":-860,"y":1780,"width":375,"height":140},
		{"id":"e7843b6f64538f02","type":"text","text":"- [Censys.io](https://search.censys.io/) :\n  good alternative to Shodan for blue teams, as it shows exposed services even on non-standard ports and provides some advanced search capabilities\n","x":-860,"y":1980,"width":375,"height":120},
		{"id":"750374ae0c1472ac","type":"text","text":"#### TLS Certificates:\n- We can use tools such as [crt.sh](https://crt.sh/) to look at TLS certificate information\n\n> Alternatively, we can investigate the certificates with Censys or [SSL Shopper](https://www.sslshopper.com/ssl-checker.html)\n","x":-1020,"y":2240,"width":440,"height":160},
		{"id":"fbc1dcb011b89c05","type":"text","text":"look out for include:\n\n- **Issuer**: A self-signed certificate is a strong indicator that the website is worth investigating\n- **Validity**: Newly-created and long-lived certificates can also indicate malware infrastructure\n- **Subject**: For self-certificates, this can point to a program behind the HTTPS (e.g., Pfsense)","x":-1400,"y":2140,"width":320,"height":260},
		{"id":"4e3ef2df0bd480da","x":107,"y":1980,"width":813,"height":540,"color":"#0008ff","type":"text","text":"## SOC Analyst Workflow\n\nAt a minimum, our workflow for IP and domain threat intel would look as follows.  \nBe mindful that some steps may be skipped or reordered depending on the alert.\n\n- **Investigate the domain**\n    - Does it \"look\" legit? Does it remind you of typosquatting?\n    - What is its reputation? Is it a known malicious domain?\n    - When was it registered? If it's new, that's suspicious.\n    - Resolve the domain to IP and investigate the IP.\n- **Investigate the IP**\n    - Is it a CDN range? If yes, no need to investigate further.\n    - What is its reputation? Use VirusTotal, AbuseIPDB, etc.\n    - Where is it located? Is it a VPN node? How does it match the alert?  \n        (Example: US-based user is not expected to log in from Asia)\n    - What AS type does it belong to? How does it match the alert?  \n        (Example: employees are not expected to log in from cloud hosting)"},
		{"id":"513fbfa88de8d1bb","type":"text","text":"- [IP2Proxy](https://www.ip2proxy.com/) \n- [Spur](https://spur.us/)","x":-1787,"y":1345,"width":235,"height":71},
		{"id":"6c2645ef3dc0c172","type":"text","text":"Services like ProtonVPN, NordVPN, and dozens of others make it trivial to fake any location in the world. Thus, the SOC's job is to answer one question: Is this a real user, or someone behind a VPN?","x":-2382,"y":1179,"width":379,"height":202,"color":"1"}
	],
